from common import *
mc('S1.1','sl','A dissolved solid','A sample contains water, dissolved sodium chloride and suspended silica. Which sequence recovers dry sodium chloride?', ['Filter, then evaporate the filtrate','Evaporate, then filter the dry residue','Distil, then evaporate the distillate','Filter, then dry the residue'],0,'Filtration removes insoluble silica. Evaporating water from the filtrate leaves sodium chloride. The first residue is silica; the distillate is water.')
mc('S1.1','sl','Heating through melting','A pure solid melts while energy is supplied at a constant rate. Its temperature remains constant. What happens to its particles during melting?', ['Their average kinetic energy rises','Their potential energy rises while average kinetic energy remains constant','Both average kinetic and potential energies fall','Their mass decreases'],1,'Energy separates particles against attractions, increasing potential energy. Constant temperature means constant average kinetic energy.')
mc('S1.2','sl','Ion inventory',r'Which ion contains 15 protons, 16 neutrons and 18 electrons?', [r'$^{31}_{15}\mathrm{P}^{3-}$',r'$^{31}_{16}\mathrm{S}^{2-}$',r'$^{33}_{15}\mathrm{P}^{3+}$',r'$^{31}_{15}\mathrm{P}^{3+}$'],0,'Atomic number 15 identifies phosphorus; mass number is 31. Three extra electrons give a charge of 3−.')
mc('S1.2','sl','An enriched isotope sample','A manufactured sample of an element contains 60.0% isotope of relative mass 10.0 and 40.0% isotope of relative mass 11.0. What is its relative atomic mass?', ['10.4','10.5','10.6','10.0'],0,'The weighted mean is 0.600×10.0+0.400×11.0=10.4. Equal weighting would ignore the specified abundances.')
mc('S1.3','sl','Copper and its ion',r'Which is the ground-state electron configuration of $\mathrm{Cu^+}$? Atomic number of Cu is 29.', [r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^{10}$',r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^94s^1$',r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^84s^2$',r'$[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^{10}4s^1$'],0,'Neutral Cu is [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹; the 4s electron is removed first, leaving [Ar]3d¹⁰.')
mc('S1.3','sl','A p-sublevel model','The diagram represents three orbitals in the same p sublevel. Which principle is violated by the shown occupation?', ['Pauli exclusion only','Hund’s rule only','Both Pauli exclusion and Hund’s rule','Neither principle'],1,'Each occupied orbital obeys Pauli exclusion, but pairing occurs before all three degenerate orbitals contain one electron.',figure=orbital([('2p',['↑↓','↑',''])]))
mc('S1.3','sl','Hydrogen radiation','An electron in a hydrogen atom drops from n=4 to n=2. Which statement is correct?', ['A photon is absorbed and the atom is ionized','A photon is emitted with energy equal to the level difference','A continuous range of photon energies is emitted in this single transition','The electron gains kinetic energy equal to an arbitrary photon energy'],1,'A transition between two specified discrete levels emits one photon whose energy equals the level separation.')
mc('S1.4','sl','Counting ions',r'How many chloride ions are present in $0.0200\ \mathrm{mol}$ of completely dissociated $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$? Use $N_A=6.02\times10^{23}\ \mathrm{mol^{-1}}$.', [r'$1.20\times10^{22}$',r'$3.61\times10^{22}$',r'$4.82\times10^{22}$',r'$6.02\times10^{23}$'],1,'There are three chloride ions per formula unit: 3×0.0200×6.02×10²³=3.61×10²².')
mc('S1.4','sl','A two-stage dilution',r'$10.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.800\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ stock solution is diluted to $100.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. Then $25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of this solution is diluted to $250.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$. What is the final concentration?', [r'$0.0800\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$',r'$0.00800\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$',r'$0.0200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$',r'$0.00200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$'],1,'Each dilution reduces concentration by a factor of 10. The overall factor is 100, giving 0.00800 mol dm⁻³.')
mc('S1.5','sl','Warming a sealed syringe','An ideal gas is held at constant pressure. Its temperature rises from 20.0°C to 80.0°C. By what factor does its volume increase?', ['4.00','1.20','0.830','1.00'],1,'Use absolute temperatures: V₂/V₁=353.15/293.15=1.2047, approximately 1.20. Celsius ratios are invalid.')
mc('S1.5','sl','Selecting an ideal-gas sample','Which sample most closely meets the assumptions of an ideal gas?', ['Helium at low pressure and high temperature','Helium near liquefaction at high pressure','Water vapour near condensation at high pressure','Ammonia near liquefaction at high pressure'],0,'Low pressure makes particle volume negligible; high temperature reduces the relative effect of attractions. Helium has weak interparticle attractions.')
mc('S1.3','ahl','A large ionization jump','An element has successive ionization energies of 590, 1150, 4940 and 6480 kJ mol⁻¹. In which group is it most likely found?', ['1','2','13','14'],1,'The large increase follows removal of two electrons. The third electron is from an inner shell, indicating two valence electrons and group 2.')
wr('S1.1','sl','Separating a plant extract','A student uses a polar silica plate and a relatively non-polar mobile phase. Two components P and Q produce the constructed chromatogram shown. The solvent front is measured from the baseline.',[(r'Calculate $R_f$ for P and Q.',[('M1','Use distance travelled by spot divided by distance travelled by solvent front.'),('A1','P: 2.0/8.0=0.25; Q: 6.0/8.0=0.75.')]),('Deduce which component interacts more strongly with silica, and explain why the starting spots must be above the solvent level.',[('R1','P travels less because it interacts more strongly with the polar stationary phase.'),('R1','Submerged spots would dissolve into the solvent reservoir instead of moving from a defined starting line.')])],kind='data-based',difficulty='medium',figure=tlc([('P',2),('Q',6)]))
wr('S1.1','sl','Testing a melting plateau','Constructed measurements for a heated sample are listed. Heating power is constant.'+table(['Time / min','Temperature / °C'],[(0,40),(1,50),(2,60),(3,60),(4,60),(5,70)]),[('Identify the time interval consistent with melting, and describe the energy changes during that interval.',[('A1','From 2 to 4 minutes.'),('R1','Average kinetic energy remains constant while potential energy increases as particle attractions are overcome.')]),('Suggest one further measurement that would strengthen the claim that the sample is pure.',[('R1','Repeat melting-point measurements with a calibrated sensor and slow heating, or compare the narrow melting range with a reliable pure-substance value. A plateau alone does not prove purity.')])],kind='data-based',difficulty='medium',figure=plot([('',[(0,40),(1,50),(2,60),(3,60),(4,60),(5,70)],'#186c93')],'Time / min','Temperature / °C',ylim=(35,75)))
wr('S1.2','sl','An isotope-labelled ion',r'A tracer is introduced as $^{34}_{16}\mathrm{S}^{2-}$.', [('State the number of protons, neutrons and electrons in the ion.',[('A1','16 protons and 18 neutrons.'),('A1','18 electrons.')]),(r'Explain why $^{32}_{16}\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ has very similar chemical behaviour but a different mass.',[('R1','Both ions have the same nuclear charge and electron configuration, which primarily determine chemical behaviour.'),('R1','The isotope with mass number 34 contains two more neutrons.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S1.2','sl','Determining isotope enrichment','An element has two isotopes of relative masses 63.0 and 65.0. An enriched sample has relative atomic mass 64.20.',[('Determine the percentage abundance of the heavier isotope.',[('M1','Let x be the heavier-isotope fraction: 63(1−x)+65x=64.20.'),('A1','x=0.600, so the heavier isotope is 60.0%.')]),('A second sample has mean relative atomic mass 63.40. Explain whether it contains a greater or smaller fraction of the lighter isotope.',[('R1','It contains more of the lighter isotope because its weighted mean is closer to 63.'),('A1','Its heavier-isotope fraction is 0.200, so its lighter-isotope fraction is 80.0%.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S1.2','ahl','Interpreting isotope peaks','A constructed mass spectrum of an element contains only the three singly charged atomic-ion peaks shown. Peak intensities are proportional to isotope abundance.',[('Calculate the relative atomic mass of the sample.',[('M1','Normalize intensities: total=100; use (24×78+25×10+26×12)/100.'),('A1','Relative atomic mass=24.34.')]),('Explain why a peak at m/z=12 would not necessarily prove the presence of a new isotope if doubly charged ions were also produced.',[('R1','An ion with relative mass 24 and charge +2 also has m/z=12.'),('R1','Charge information is needed to distinguish that ion from a singly charged isotope with mass 12.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(24,78),(25,10),(26,12)],'#186c93')],'m/z','Relative intensity',xlim=(23,27),ylim=(0,100),sticks=True,annotate=True))
wr('S1.3','sl','Comparing atomic emissions','Two discharge lamps emit narrow lines. Lamp A emits at 480 nm; lamp B emits at 640 nm.',[('Identify which radiation has higher frequency and higher photon energy, explaining both choices without calculation.',[('R1','A has higher frequency because wavelength and frequency are inversely related.'),('R1','A has higher photon energy because photon energy increases with frequency.')]),('Explain why a line spectrum supports a model of discrete electron energy levels.',[('R1','Only particular photon energies are observed.'),('R1','They correspond to allowed differences between discrete electron levels, rather than a continuous range of energies.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S1.3','sl','Chromium and bromine',r'The atomic numbers of chromium and bromine are 24 and 35.',[('Write their ground-state electron configurations using noble-gas shorthand.',[('A1',r'Cr: $[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^54s^1$.'),('A1',r'Br: $[\mathrm{Ar}]3d^{10}4s^24p^5$.')]),('State the number of unpaired electrons in each neutral atom.',[('A1','Cr has 6 unpaired electrons.'),('A1','Br has 1 unpaired electron.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S1.3','ahl','A convergence limit',r'The convergence wavelength for transitions to the ground state of a gaseous atom is $250.0\ \mathrm{nm}$. Use $h=6.63\times10^{-34}\ \mathrm{J\,s}$, $c=3.00\times10^8\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and $N_A=6.02\times10^{23}\ \mathrm{mol^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate the first ionization energy in kJ mol⁻¹.',[('M1',r'$E=hc/\lambda=(6.63\times10^{-34})(3.00\times10^8)/(250.0\times10^{-9})=7.956\times10^{-19}\ \mathrm{J}$ per atom.'),('M1','Multiply by Avogadro’s constant and divide by 1000.'),('A1','First ionization energy = 479 kJ mol⁻¹ (3 significant figures).')]),('Explain what happens to the electron at the convergence limit.',[('R1','It reaches the boundary of the bound states and is removed from the atom; the transition corresponds to ionization from the ground state.')])])
wr('S1.3','ahl','Locating an ionization jump','The following constructed ionization data belong to a main-group element.'+table(['Electron removed','IE / kJ mol⁻¹'],[(1,800),(2,1600),(3,2800),(4,4400),(5,16000),(6,20000)]),[('Deduce the group of the element and justify your answer.',[('A1','Group 14.'),('R1','The large jump is after four electrons have been removed; the fifth is removed from an inner shell.')]),('Explain why all successive ionization energies increase, even before the large jump.',[('R1','Each electron is removed from an increasingly positive ion.'),('R1','Remaining electrons experience stronger attraction / reduced electron–electron repulsion, so removal requires more energy.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(1,800),(2,1600),(3,2800),(4,4400),(5,16000),(6,20000)],'#186c93')],'Electron removed','IE / kJ mol⁻¹',ylim=(0,22000)))
wr('S1.4','sl','Hydrate from constant mass',r'A $2.460\ \mathrm{g}$ sample of $\mathrm{MgSO_4}\cdot x\mathrm{H_2O}$ is heated to constant mass. The anhydrous residue has mass $1.200\ \mathrm{g}$. Use molar masses $120.4\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{MgSO_4}$ and $18.02\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ for water.',[('Determine x.',[('M1','Water lost=1.260 g; n(water)=1.260/18.02=0.06992 mol.'),('M1','n(MgSO₄)=1.200/120.4=0.009967 mol.'),('A1','Ratio=7.02; x=7.')]),('Predict the effect on the calculated x if some salt decomposes and releases an additional gas.',[('R1','The extra mass loss is wrongly assigned to water and residue mass is lower, so the calculated water-to-salt ratio and x are too high.')])])
wr('S1.4','sl','Preparing a copper solution',r'A technician must prepare $250.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.0400\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ copper(II) sulfate from $\mathrm{CuSO_4\cdot5H_2O}$, molar mass $249.7\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate the mass of crystals required.',[('M1','n=0.0400×0.2500=0.0100 mol.'),('A1','m=0.0100×249.7=2.50 g (3 significant figures).')]),('Describe two precautions that prevent loss of solute or an incorrect final volume.',[('R1','Transfer quantitatively, rinsing the weighing vessel/funnel and adding the washings to the volumetric flask.'),('R1','Dissolve fully and cool to the calibration temperature before making the meniscus up to the mark; mix thoroughly.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('S1.4','sl','Molecular formula from composition','A compound contains 54.5% carbon, 9.1% hydrogen and 36.4% oxygen by mass. Its molar mass is 88.0 g mol⁻¹. Use C=12.0, H=1.00 and O=16.0.',[('Determine the empirical formula and molecular formula.',[('M1','For 100 g, mole ratios are 54.5/12=4.54, 9.1/1=9.1 and 36.4/16=2.275.'),('A1','Dividing by the smallest gives 2:4:1, so empirical formula C₂H₄O.'),('A1','Empirical-formula mass=44.0; molecular formula C₄H₈O₂.')]),('Explain why the percentage composition alone cannot distinguish the molecular formula from the empirical formula.',[('R1','All integer multiples of an empirical formula have the same element mass percentages; a molar mass is also required.')])])
wr('S1.4','sl','Volumetric uncertainty',r'A pipette delivers $20.00\pm0.03\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of a stock solution into a $200.0\pm0.2\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ volumetric flask. The stock concentration is treated as exact. Use addition of percentage uncertainties for this quotient.',[('Calculate the percentage uncertainty in the diluted concentration.',[('M1','Pipette: 0.03/20.00×100=0.15%; flask: 0.2/200.0×100=0.10%.'),('A1','Combined maximum percentage uncertainty=0.25%.')]),('The diluted concentration is 0.0500 mol dm⁻³. Determine its absolute uncertainty, and identify the larger relative contribution.',[('A1','Absolute uncertainty=0.0500×0.0025=0.000125 mol dm⁻³, approximately 0.0001 mol dm⁻³.'),('A1','The pipette contributes the larger relative uncertainty.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('S1.5','sl','A gas-filled sensor',r'A sensor contains $0.0120\ \mathrm{mol}$ of gas in $300.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ at $35.0^\circ\mathrm{C}$. Treat the gas as ideal and use $R=8.31\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate the gas pressure in kPa.',[('M1','T=308.15 K and V=3.000×10⁻⁴ m³.'),('M1','p=nRT/V.'),('A1','p=102 kPa (102.43066… kPa before rounding).')]),('Explain why using 35.0 in place of the absolute temperature gives an invalid result.',[('R1','The ideal-gas relationship uses temperature measured from absolute zero, because particle kinetic energy is proportional to absolute temperature.')])])
wr('S1.5','sl','Identifying a volatile liquid',r'A volatile liquid forms $125\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of ideal gas at $100.0\ \mathrm{kPa}$ and $373\ \mathrm{K}$. The vapour has mass $0.185\ \mathrm{g}$. Use $R=8.31\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.',[('Determine the molar mass of the liquid.',[('M1','n=pV/RT=(100000×125×10⁻⁶)/(8.31×373)=0.0040327 mol.'),('A1','M=0.185/0.0040327=45.9 g mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain the effect on the inferred molar mass if air remains in the measured gas volume but its mass is not included.',[('R1','The calculated amount of liquid vapour is overestimated because the total gas includes air.'),('R1','Dividing the measured liquid mass by this excessive amount gives a molar mass that is too low.')])])
wr('S1.5','sl','A pressure–volume investigation','Constructed measurements are made at constant temperature for a fixed amount of gas.'+table(['V / dm³','p / kPa'],[(0.50,240),(0.75,160),(1.00,120),(1.50,80)]),[('Show that the data are consistent with an ideal gas.',[('M1','Compute pV for at least two pairs.'),('A1','Each pair gives 120 kPa dm³; p is inversely proportional to V at fixed n and T.')]),('Describe a linear plot that could be used to test the relationship, and give the expected intercept.',[('A1','Plot p against 1/V, or V against 1/p.'),('A1','A straight line through the origin is expected for the ideal model.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(0.5,240),(0.75,160),(1,120),(1.5,80)],'#186c93')],'Volume / dm³','Pressure / kPa',ylim=(0,260)))
wr('S1.5','sl','A leaking sample cell',r'A rigid cell initially contains gas at $150\ \mathrm{kPa}$ and $300\ \mathrm{K}$. After warming to $360\ \mathrm{K}$, its pressure is only $144\ \mathrm{kPa}$. Assume ideal behaviour.',[('Calculate the fraction of the original amount of gas remaining.',[('M1','At fixed V, n₂/n₁=(p₂/T₂)/(p₁/T₁).'),('A1','n₂/n₁=(144/360)/(150/300)=0.800; 80.0% remains.')]),('Explain why warming alone cannot account for the observation.',[('R1','With no loss, pressure would rise to 150×360/300=180 kPa.'),('R1','The lower observed pressure requires a reduction in amount of gas, consistent with leakage.')])])
wr('S1.3','ahl','Two convergence thresholds',r'The convergence frequencies for ground-state emission series of atoms X and Y are $1.20\times10^{15}$ and $1.80\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$.',[('Determine the ratio of their first ionization energies, IE(Y)/IE(X), and explain its significance.',[('M1','E=hf, so the energy ratio equals the frequency ratio.'),('A1','IE(Y)/IE(X)=1.50; more energy is needed to remove an electron from Y.')]),('A student claims this proves that Y has the smaller atomic radius. Evaluate the claim.',[('R1','The claim is not proved by these frequencies alone.'),('R1','Ionization energy also depends on nuclear charge, shielding, sublevel energy and electron pairing; additional structural information is needed.')])])
wr('S1.2','ahl','Resolving isotope abundances','A constructed atomic mass spectrum has peaks at m/z=28, 29 and 30, with intensities 92.0, 5.0 and 3.0 respectively. All ions are singly charged.',[('Calculate the relative atomic mass, treating peak positions as isotope relative masses.',[('M1','Use (28×92+29×5+30×3)/100.'),('A1','Relative atomic mass=28.11.')]),('Explain why a spectrum drawn with the highest peak set to 100 gives the same relative atomic mass after correct processing.',[('R1','Multiplying all intensities by the same factor preserves their ratios.'),('R1','Normalize by the sum of the displayed intensities before calculating the weighted mean.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(28,92),(29,5),(30,3)],'#186c93')],'m/z','Relative intensity',xlim=(27,31),ylim=(0,100),sticks=True,annotate=True))
wr('S1.4','sl','A stock-label discrepancy',r'A bottle is labelled $0.150\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ sodium chloride. Evaporation of a $20.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ sample leaves $0.1690\ \mathrm{g}$ of dry NaCl. Use $M(\mathrm{NaCl})=58.44\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. Assume no other solute.',[('Calculate the concentration found by evaporation and its percentage difference below the label value.',[('M1','n=0.1690/58.44=0.0028919 mol; c=n/0.02000.'),('A1','c=0.1446 mol dm⁻³.'),('A1','Difference=(0.150−0.144593)/0.150×100=3.60%.')]),('Would failure to dry the residue completely explain this lower result? Justify your answer.',[('R1','No. Remaining water adds to the residue mass and makes the calculated concentration too high, not too low.')])],kind='data-based')
save('S1')
